<header><p class="st-kicker">republic · Caucasus</p><h1 class="st-title">Azerbaijan Democratic Republic</h1><p class="st-meta">1918 CE – 1920 CE · capital Baku</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">The first parliamentary republic in the Muslim world, founded in 1918, which granted women the vote before it fell to the Red Army in 1920.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>As the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/transcaucasian-democratic-federative-republic">Transcaucasian Democratic Federative Republic</a> dissolved, the Azerbaijani National Council declared independence in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tbilisi">Tiflis</a> on 28 May 1918. Its government moved to <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/ganja-khanate">Ganja</a> and, with the help of the Ottoman Army of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/islam">Islam</a>, took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baku">Baku</a> from the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/centrocaspian-dictatorship">Centrocaspian Dictatorship</a> in September 1918, making it the capital.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The republic was led by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/musavat">Musavat</a> party under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mammad-amin-rasulzade">Mammad Amin Rasulzade</a> and prime ministers such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fatali-khan-khoyski">Fatali Khan Khoyski</a>, with a multi-party parliament that included minority representatives. It granted women the vote in 1919, founded <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baku">Baku</a> State University and adopted the blue, red and green flag. Baku's oil was its economic base, but it faced conflicts with <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/armenia">Armenia</a> over <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/karabakh">Karabakh</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zangezur">Zangezur</a>.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>In April 1920 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/red-army">Red Army</a> invaded, and the government ceded power to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/communist-party-of-the-soviet-union">Bolsheviks</a>, creating the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/azerbaijan-ssr">Azerbaijan SSR</a>. The independent <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/azerbaijan">Republic of Azerbaijan</a> in 1991 adopted its flag and declared itself its successor.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/azerbaijan-democratic-republic.jpg" alt="Azerbaijan Democratic Republic" width="320" height="160"><figcaption>Image: Zscout370 · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_the_Democratic_Republic_of_Azerbaijan_(1918).svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1918 CE – 1920 CE</dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>republic</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>bureaucratic</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Baku</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Mammad Amin Rasulzade, Fatali Khan Khoyski, Nasib Yusifbeyli</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Shia and Sunni Islam</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Azerbaijani</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd>Azerbaijan</dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Azerbaijan People's Republic, ADR</dd></dl></aside></div>
